\begin{proof}
Let $G$ be a group with $|G|=462=11\cdot42$. By the Sylow theorems, a Sylow $11$-subgroup $P$ exists and has order $11$. If $n_{11}$ is the number of Sylow $11$-subgroups, then
\[
n_{11}\mid42\quad	ext{and}
\quad n_{11}\equiv1\pmod{11}.
\]
The divisors of $42$ are $1,2,3,6,7,14,21,42$, and only $1$ is congruent to $1$ modulo $11$. Thus $n_{11}=1$. Hence $P$ is the unique Sylow $11$-subgroup and is normal in $G$. Since $P$ has order $11$, it is neither trivial nor equal to $G$ because $1<11<462$. Therefore $G$ has a nontrivial proper normal subgroup, so $G$ is not simple.
\end{proof}
